  "query_preview": "/Thursday through Monday/...\nIssued 312 AM MDT MON JUL 27 2026\nSouthwest flow aloft will continue to be the name of the game \nin the long-term period. With high pressure aloft over the Four \nCorners region, and troughing over British Columbia, conditions \nwill remain warm, dry, and breezy. Later in the week, the high \npressure aloft will shift over the Great Basin, allowing for \ntemperatures to rise to around 5 degrees above normal Thursday \nand after. Afternoon breezes will increase come Saturday and \nSunday, as a shortwave associated with the aforementioned trough\nmoves inland and tightens the pressure gradient over our area. \nA dry cold front associated with the shortwave will sag south \ncome this weekend, allowing for temperatures to cool to near, or\neven slightly below, normal Sunday and Monday. Otherwise, the \nregion is expected to remain considerably drier than normal \nthroughout the long-term period.",
